mbination outwards. Resistances add in series; conductances 1/R add in parallel."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:b.ii":{"hint":"Mark the potential difference across the component and the current through it. Are these the quantities given?","notes":["For a resistor, V = IR. Series components carry the same current; parallel branches have the same potential difference.","Simplify a resistor network from the innermost series or parallel combination outwards. Resistances add in series; conductances 1/R add in parallel."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:c.i":{"hint":"Work out what stays fixed when the circuit changes: voltage, current, or neither.","notes":["Electrical power is P = VI. For a resistor this can also be I²R or V²/R; choose the form using known quantities.","Energy transferred is Pt for constant power. A filament lamp’s resistance can change with temperature, so do not assume constant resistance unless stated."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:c.ii":{"hint":"Work out what stays fixed when the circuit changes: voltage, current, or neither.","notes":["Electrical power is P = VI. For a resistor this can also be I²R or V²/R; choose the form using known quantities.","Energy transferred is Pt for constant power. A filament lamp’s resistance can change with temperature, so do not assume constant resistance unless stated."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:d.i":{"hint":"Distinguish the energy supplied per coulomb by the source from the voltage available across its terminals.","notes":["For a discharging cell, ε = I(R + r) and terminal voltage V = ε − Ir. Internal resistance dissipates some of the source’s power.","A graph of terminal V against I has intercept ε and gradient −r. Check whether the cell is delivering or receiving current before using the voltage sign."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:d.ii":{"hint":"Distinguish the energy supplied per coulomb by the source from the voltage available across its terminals.","notes":["For a discharging cell, ε = I(R + r) and terminal voltage V = ε − Ir. Internal resistance dissipates some of the source’s power.","A graph of terminal V against I has intercept ε and gradient −r. Check whether the cell is delivering or receiving current before using the voltage sign."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:e.i":{"hint":"Mark the potential difference across the component and the current through it. Are these the quantities given?","notes":["For a resistor, V = IR. Series components carry the same current; parallel branches have the same potential difference.","Simplify a resistor network from the innermost series or parallel combination outwards. Resistances add in series; conductances 1/R add in parallel.","Link the relevant physical cause to the observation asked about. Naming a law alone does not explain why this particular result follows."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:e.ii":{"hint":"Distinguish the energy supplied per coulomb by the source from the voltage available across its terminals.","notes":["For a discharging cell, ε = I(R + r) and terminal voltage V = ε − Ir. Internal resistance dissipates some of the source’s power.","A graph of terminal V against I has intercept ε and gradient −r. Check whether the cell is delivering or receiving current before using the voltage sign.","Link the relevant physical cause to the observation asked about. Naming a law alone does not explain why this particular result follows."]},"sup-27a882d79901fd58-w1:e.iii":{"hint":"Work out what stays fixed when the circuit changes: voltage, current, or neither.","notes":["Electrical power is P = VI. For a resistor this can also be I²R or V²/R; choose the form using known quantities.","Energy transferred is Pt for constant power. A filament lamp’s resistance can change with temperature, so do not assume constant resistance unless stated."]}}},"answers":{"entries":{}},"presentation":{"practice/supplement/sup-27a882d79901fd58-1-a-785c973af798bc40.webp":{"crop":[0,1,1124,101],"size":[1124,128]},"practice/supplement/sup-27a882d79901fd58-1-b.i-68e827ab23